  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Chat models end their turn by generating a special end-of-turn token, which the app does not display; e.g. Meta documents that Llama 3 signals the end of the assistant message by generating <|eot_id|>. OpenAI's exact token for current ChatGPT models is not public; the mechanism is the same for chat LLMs.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "The end token is part of the model's vocabulary and is scored and sampled at each step like any other token; generation halts when it is produced.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "type": "illustrative",
      "text": "Probabilities shown (end token ~0.1% mid-sentence, ~90% after a complete answer, 'Also' 6%) are invented to show the shape.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "In chat-format training data, every assistant turn ends with the end-of-turn token, which is how the model learns where answers end.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "With sampling, a high-probability token can still lose the draw, so a model can continue past a natural ending.",
      "sources": [
        "S3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Apps and APIs also cap output length; when the cap is reached, generation stops even mid-sentence (finish_reason 'length' vs 'stop').",
      "sources": [
        "S4",
        "S5"
      ]
    }
  ],
